{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1495861-running-unorthodox-challenges","id":1495861,"title":"Running unorthodox challenges","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-10-29T19:32:15-07:00","id":1495861,"title":"Running unorthodox challenges","content":"Between the three major stat categories of Physical, Social &amp; Mental, the latter one is definitely my weakest aspect whenever I ST. Coming up with plots about artifacts here or researching curses there or navigating political drama otherwhere is one thing; I probably could be better at making a web but I suspect I can manage the basics at least. But actually making it something engaging from a gameplay perspective instead of just making extended rolls to hit a goal or the likes, which in my view becomes a one-trick pony fairly quick.<br /> <br /> And so I turn to you fine people for pro-tips on how else I can potentially approach Mental-focused challenges for my players to engage with, whether they're researching, investigating, using occult know-how or scientific knowledge that probably goes beyond the head of any real person at the table, in a more interesting manner that is simultaneously fair to the presumably good rating in the relevant stats and which doesn't leave the player with either too complete or too incomplete information to work with based off a roll or a few. Tall and vague order, I know. I'll take what I get; if nothing else it's food for thought.<br /> <br /> While an aside from my main interest, I'm also curious to hear what people might think about unique and/or interesting approaches to Social and Physical challenges.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7060-karavolos","name":"Karavolos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7060_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"163"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-30T14:39:30-07:00","id":1495910,"title":"","content":"In the case of occult, you could use metaphors. There is a D&amp;D game I am in where one character reached a hall in a quantumly unstable castle that had paper cranes, charms, dream catchers and so on, a hall of dreams. When he closed his eyes with the intent to see (key detail to accomplish it), he saw the hall of nightmares. He had to leave due to extreme danger, but in hindsight he wanted to try to see with one eye open and one closed, to see what is perceived when they are in balance.<br /> <br /> You could also make escape rooms, make an area require lateral thinking to solve. In the same game, we had to enter a mansion made by a cosmic being that was Sherlock Holmes meets John Constantine. We had to enter it by deducing the keypad password from an unfinished chess game and our knowledge of the situation it represented in his own lifetime. Then we had to solve another puzzle by ordering the mail delivery by date (first item on the stack was 5th in the correct order, so first number is 5 and so on). Inside there was a letter that some of us suspected had explosive runes, but the trick was a weight switch on the small table it was in, so we had to swap it Indiana Jones style. After that we had to solve a statues puzzle, which made the room drift between temperature extremes before stabilizing, with the final temperature being the password. Afterwards a party member had to disguise himself as a cosmic being the owner was looking for and behave like him for the guardian golems to let us pass.<br /> <br /> We met a more intelligent golem that wanted to help in exchange for freedom, but he couldn't access all his data. We had to use potions that temporarily increased or decreased his intelligence to get the different tidbits, since we only had a few of each, we needed to make the questions count. We then had to choose the order we would visit the remaining rooms according to match the events in the owner's life and, when we found treasure, we needed to decide what to take. Each item had a metaphysical weight in itself, including the golem, so figuring out what to take exactly was a puzzle in itself. If we tried to take more than the weight limit, failed or timed out any puzzle in the mansion, then the house and the entire city block would be hit by a fiery explosion made by weaponizing the fundamental force of Truth (causing resistant damage). By the time we left successfully, the house and the entire block teleported out. We were dealing with occult forces way beyond common sense and matching wits with the legacy of one of the smartest (and most paranoid) beings that had ever existed. There were almost no rolls, it was completely player driven, just thinking through the puzzles and using story clues to piece things together, it was great experience that made an impression and felt like a fair challenge of the player's and character's deductive skills.<br /> <br /> I also recommend the <i>Chronicles of Darkness </i>book, the section about the God-Machine, how to approach an investigation of its activities and how it escalates things is great. It has its own byzantine web of intrigue, built backdoors into the laws of reality and obscure goals. Let players have clues via roles, but make putting them together something that has to be done the old fashioned way. Make NPCs give them ideas on how to experiment, question how to do it safely or even which expert to call for a greater picture, each ally or contact helping them see one more aspect of the whole, but no one knowing as much as the party to piece it all together. This may lead to scenes where players and NPCs are bouncing ideas off each other, which can lead to eureka moments and the creation of plans happening organically.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>New experiences are the font of creativity, when seeking inspiration, break your routine.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1278855-the-agathos-kai-sophos-a-acanthus-legacy-of-strategists\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#1155CC\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Agathos Kai Sophos</span></span></a><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, an Acanthus Legacy of strategists (Mind/Time)</span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1360568-the-szary-stra%C5%BCnik-an-obrimos-legacy-of-scholars-of-the-glyphs-of-fate\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#1155CC\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Szary Stra\u017cnik</span></span></a><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, an Obrimos Legacy whose invisible hands guide through the Glyphs of Fate (Fate/Prime)</span>","edited":{"userid":24280,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24280-kaiserafini","user":"KaiserAfini","date":". 10-30-2022, 06:40 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24280-kaiserafini","name":"KaiserAfini","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24280_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2648"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}